From 5e1e37de656effe29fa7ce5ce00dccbed1cb6f5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Casey <4348807+justincasey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:15:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] coop MoE decode: unpredicated slice loop, early weight
 prefetch, device-side split-k

Slice loop (bit-identical): A fragments run AD=2 slices ahead in a ring like the
weights; weight and A fetch pointers advance and clamp at the last slice so every
fetch is unconditional (no predicated loads, no ring MOVs, no local-memory stack);
lanes past the run's rows read its last row and the MMA's upper 8 rows are left
undefined (rows are independent, only rows < nrows reach sh_red) instead of
re-zeroed each slice; the 3 bpw path's idle lanes read lane 23's word.

Block start: the run table packs each run's local expert above its start, so a
block reaches its weights' pointer table after one dependent load; gemv_tile
issues the weight prefetch before the caller's remaining setup (bsz-1 input
rotation, now only the chunks of the split's k range) and the rows barrier.

Split-k chosen per launch on the device from the run count (the host-sized grid
can't see the routing): the gate/up stage splits the items of a launch's last,
part-filled wave so they fill one wave (launches of up to three waves; split
partials fp32 for the whole launch), the down stage splits a launch whose items
don't fill one wave. EXL3_MOE_COOP_DSPLIT caps it (1 disables), EXL3_MOE_COOP_KSPLIT
still forces a static split. The 3 bpw kernels are held to two blocks per SM.

GB10, TP2-rank MiniMax geometry (6144/1536, 128 experts, top-4, mixed-K split
112xK2 / 16xK3 down), L2 flushed, per-layer coop kernel time:
  live c=1 routing (4 rows):  445 -> 414 us (169 -> 182 GB/s)
  live c=4 routing (16 rows): 1045 -> 1010 us
  16 distinct experts, 4 rows: 608 -> 593 us (bit-identical)
Unsplit launches are bit-identical; split ones stay within the coop tests'
tolerances (bench_moe_coop_split: vs mixedk <= 9.1e-4, vs fp32 ref 6.4e-4).
